        "inputText": "# Oral Histories with Conflicting Accounts\n\n## Abstract\n\nA concise oral-history summary can make a disputed event appear settled if it merges different speakers into a single account. We propose comparing ordinary concise summaries with summaries that retain attribution when accounts conflict. The collection concerns a factory closure for which two speakers give different dates [S1]. The disagreement will remain part of the evidence rather than become an error for the summarizer to fix.\n\n## Introduction\n\nInterview transcripts record what a speaker says, not an automatically verified history of the event. The supplied collection uses pseudonyms, and no archival source has resolved the competing closure dates [S1]. Consequently, a summary should distinguish the absence of corroboration from the absence of an event and avoid assigning a motive that the speaker did not state.\n\n## Related Work\n\nThe collection brief identifies unresolved disagreement and makes speaker attribution necessary for interpreting it [S1]. Disputed dates must remain attached to the speakers who supplied them [S2]. We will use this constraint to test whether a shorter account remains faithful to the structure of the evidence, rather than judge it only by how smoothly the narrative reads.\n\n## Methodology\n\nWe will summarize 16 interview excerpts under both conditions [S2]. In the ordinary condition, we will request a concise account. In the attribution-preserving condition, we will explicitly retain any disagreement. Reviewers will receive the source excerpts and identify any statement that assigns a date, motive, or event to the wrong speaker. The pseudonyms will be used consistently in both versions.\n\n## Planned Evaluation\n\nEvaluation will assess readability, attribution accuracy, and the handling of unresolved dates. We will include cases where a summary is readable but merges two accounts. Additionally, reviewers will check if a silence in the transcript is incorrectly interpreted as a negative historical claim. Reviewers will not be asked to determine the true closure date based on evidence that does not establish it.\n\n## Limitations and Conclusion\n\nThe study cannot resolve the underlying historical disagreement. Consequently, a faithful summary may be less tidy than a single chronological narrative. We will not reconcile the dates or infer motives without new evidence [S2]. The intended benefit is a clearer account of who said what and where the source packet still leaves the history uncertain.",
